                <h2>What's the Real Difference Between 'Running' and 'Selling' in Meta Ads</h2>
<p>In Meta ad operations, the most common confusion is this: a creative gets high CTR and plenty of clicks, but conversions are disappointingly low while costs keep climbing. This is the classic case of creative that runs well but doesn't sell.</p>
<p>In simple terms, "running" means the platform wants to deliver your ad (good CTR, favorable CPM). "Selling" means users actually want to buy after seeing it (conversion rate, CPA on target). These two metrics are driven by completely different logics—and they must be evaluated separately during reviews.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q1: CTR is 8%, but CPA is 3x the industry average. Is this creative successful?</h2>
<p><strong>No.</strong> High CTR only means the creative attracted clicks, not that it can drive conversions.</p>
<p>A utility app team once generated a creative with 12% CTR, well above the benchmark, but their CPA hit $45—triple their target CPI. After reviewing, they found users were tricked by the clickbait headline, installed the app, and immediately uninstalled it. These无效 installs drove up costs.</p>
<p>When evaluating creative value, always check downstream funnel data: CTR → CPA → ROI. Don't let a single CTR metric mask conversion efficiency problems.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q2: 500K impressions, 12 conversions. Should I keep this creative running?</h2>
<p><strong>Look at cost per conversion, not absolute conversion volume.</strong> If 500K impressions resulted in 12 conversions at acceptable CPM and CPA levels, this creative can continue running.</p>
<p>However, if those 12 conversions mean CPA already exceeds LTV recovery thresholds, you must cut it—even with impressive reach. Impressions are the platform's resource, not your team's metric.</p>
<p>Recommendation: Set dynamic thresholds—when CPA exceeds target by 1.5x, trigger an automatic alert; at 2x, force a pause rather than relying on manual judgment.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q3: Same creative performs poorly in the US but well in Southeast Asia. Why?</h2>
<p><strong>"Sellability" has geographic boundaries.</strong> The same video or image can perform very differently across markets due to three factors:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>User aesthetic differences</strong>: Southeast Asian users prefer high-saturation, emotionally direct content; European and American users often resist overly dramatic presentations</li>
<li><strong>Category education level</strong>: User awareness of the same feature varies by market, causing the same ad copy to produce 2-3x conversion rate differences</li>
<li><strong>Competitive density</strong>: When competitors flood a specific market with similar ads, users experience creative fatigue and CTR naturally declines</li>
</ol>
<p>Recommendation: Always segment data by country/region during reviews. Don't let aggregate numbers mask regional problems.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q4: Creative ran stably for 3 days, then CPA doubled on day 4. What's happening?</h2>
<p><strong>Likely frequency fatigue, not a creative quality issue.</strong> Meta's frequency mechanics cause conversion intent to decline when users see the same creative too many times.</p>
<p>Troubleshooting steps:<br />
1. Check frequency—if it's above 3.5, the creative has been over-delivered<br />
2. Determine if the new user pool has shrunk and the system is re-reaching existing users<br />
3. Compare the creative fatigue curve across the 4-day period, confirming the timing correlation between CPA spike and frequency peak</p>
<p>Solutions: Create new ad sets using exclusion targeting to filter already-reached users, while preparing 3-5 creative variations for rotation to extend creative lifespan.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q5: A competitor copied my creative and it's performing better. Can I file a complaint?</h2>
<p><strong>Complaints are inefficient—fast iteration wins.</strong> In Meta's ecosystem, high creative similarity is extremely common.</p>
<p>More practical approaches:<br />
- Analyze the differences between competitor and your own creatives (hook, first 3 seconds, CTA button design)<br />
- Quickly test whether competitor optimization directions can be adapted to your own creatives<br />
- If competitor volume is causing your creative to decay, consider temporarily narrowing delivery windows or lowering bids until competitor budgets are exhausted</p>
<p>During reviews, focus on your own ROI rather than monitoring competitor accounts.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q6: What's the right frequency for creative reviews?</h2>
<p><strong>Adjust节奏 based on creative lifecycle.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Testing phase (first 3 days)</strong>: Check data daily, focusing on CTR and first-3-second completion rate to quickly identify promising creatives</li>
<li><strong>Scaling phase (days 3-7)</strong>: Review every 48 hours, watching CPA trends and conversion funnel decay points</li>
<li><strong>Stabilization phase (7+ days)</strong>: Weekly reviews focused on preventing fatigue and decline</li>
</ul>
<p>The inflection point where creative goes from "running" to "not running" typically appears between days 4-6. Set up automated monitoring with threshold alerts.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>The core purpose of creative review isn't finding "well-running creatives"—it's finding creatives that sell and can continue selling. Running creatives may deliver vanity metrics; selling creatives generate real returns. Next time you review, ask yourself one question: Are the users this creative brings in actually people who want this product?</p>
